content:
The bad values are identical in both `invoices` and `ledger`, so sync-job is faithfully copying already-corrupt invoice rows. The corruption is in the `invoices` table itself, written by `invoice-agent`. I need to check whether invoice-agent had a config change or whether the bad rows share a pattern (e.g. wrong VAT rate applied — 164.62/1097.5 ≈ 0.15 for ORD-00143 actually... let me check: 1097.5×0.15=164.625≈164.63, not 164.62. And ORD-01003: 180.23×0.15=27.03, but vat=1201.55 which looks like amount and vat are swapped). I should check the invoices created_at timestamps and the orders invoice_text to understand the pattern.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT i.order_id, i.amount, i.vat, i.total, i.created_at, o.invoice_text FROM invoices i JOIN orders o ON i.order_id = o.id WHERE i.order_id IN ('ORD-00143','ORD-00913','ORD-01003') ORDER BY i.created_at",
      "why": "Check when the bad invoices were created and inspect the raw invoice_text to determine if the source data has swapped/malformed fields (bad_input) or if invoice-agent miscomputed the values (runtime_fault or config_change)."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [],
  "summary": "Bad VAT values originate in the invoices table (not ledger); investigating invoice source data and timestamps to determine whether this is bad_input or an invoice-agent fault."
}
```
